 "source": "Laozi 老子, 道德經 (Daodejing), chapter 38, in the Wang Bi 王弼 recension (華亭張氏原本, 晉 王弼注), as transcribed at Chinese Wikisource; verified character-for-character against the printed public-domain edition of Paul Carus, Lao-Tze's Tao-Teh-King: Chinese-English (Chicago: Open Court, 1898), p. 71. All punctuation is editorial — the original has none — and the division into 81 chapters is traditional. Sense-units follow Wang Bi's own commentary lemmata. Translations are made fresh from the Chinese. Pronunciation convention: modern Mandarin, given in Hanyu Pinyin — an admitted anachronism, and the living convention for reading these texts. Where an Old Chinese rhyme is invisible in Mandarin, a note says so. Prepared audio is synthesized speech in that same convention (cmn-CN-Chirp3-HD-Achernar).",
 "about": "Chapter 38 of the Daodejing. Its traditional title is 論德 (Discoursing on Potency). The chapter titles are not part of the text and are not Wang Bi's: they come from the Heshanggong 河上公 recension and are carried here only as navigation labels, so a chapter can be found by something other than its number. The received text is Wang Bi's (3rd c. AD); the manuscripts found at Mawangdui (1973) and Guodian (1993) are older and differ in places, and the notes flag those differences where they matter. Classical Chinese has no inflection, no obligatory subject, and no fixed parts of speech — the notes show how the line produces its meaning. Read the characters; the pinyin under each one is a reading aid you can hide with the transcription button.",
 "sentences": [
  {
   "t": "上德不德，\n是以有德；\n下德不失德，\n是以無德。\n上德無為而無以為，\n下德為之而有以為。\n上仁為之而無以為，\n上義為之而有以為。\n上禮為之而莫之應， \n則攘臂而扔之。\n故失道而後德，\n失德而後仁，\n失仁而後義，\n失義而後禮。\n夫禮者，\n忠信之薄，\n而亂之首。\n前識者，\n道之華，\n而愚之始。\n是以大丈夫處其厚，\n不居其薄；\n處其實，\n不居其華。\n故去彼取此。",
