   "l": "The highest potency does not hold itself potent:\ntherefore it has potency;\nthe lower potency never lets potency go:\ntherefore it is without potency.\nThe highest potency acts without forcing, and has nothing it acts for;\nthe lower potency acts upon things, and has something it acts for.\nThe highest benevolence acts upon things, and has nothing it acts for;\nthe highest rightness acts upon things, and has something it acts for.\nThe highest ritual acts upon things, and nothing answers it:\nthen it bares the arm and hauls them along.\nSo: the Way once lost, then potency;\npotency once lost, then benevolence;\nbenevolence once lost, then rightness;\nrightness once lost, then the rites.\nNow the rites —\nthe thinning of loyalty and good faith,\nand the head of disorder.\nForeknowledge —\nthe flower of the Way,\nand the beginning of stupidity.\nTherefore the great man dwells in the thick,\nand does not settle in the thin;\ndwells in the fruit,\nand does not settle in the flower.\nSo he puts that away and takes this.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "The highest potency does not hold itself to be potent, and so it has potency; the lower potency never lets go of its potency, and so it has none. The highest potency acts without forcing, and has no purpose in acting; the lower potency works upon things, and has a purpose in acting. The highest benevolence works upon things, and has no purpose in acting; the highest rightness works upon things, and has a purpose in acting. The highest ritual works upon things, and when nothing answers it, rolls up its sleeve and hauls them into line. So: the Way is lost, and then there is potency; potency is lost, and then benevolence; benevolence is lost, and then rightness; rightness is lost, and then the rites. The rites are loyalty and good faith worn thin, and the head of disorder; foreknowledge is the flowering of the Way, and the beginning of stupidity. And so the man of stature dwells in what is thick and not in what is thin, in the fruit and not in the flower. He puts the one away and takes the other.",
   "n": "This chapter opens the second half of the book, the 德經 or Book of Potency — and in the Mawangdui silk manuscripts it opens the book outright, the whole 德 half standing before the 道 half. Wang Bi's commentary runs the entire chapter as one lemma, which is why it is here as a single sense-unit: the argument is one long descent and he will not cut it.\n\nFive things to watch. First, 德 as a verb: 上德不德 is 'highest potency does not potency', the noun pressed into service as a verb, and it means holding oneself to be potent, making a display of the thing. Wang Bi says it plainly — the man of highest potency uses only the Way, does not treat his potency as potency, holds nothing and uses nothing, and so has it. Second, 上 and 下 are grades, not places: the top and bottom of a scale, as in 太上 in chapter 17. Third, 無以為 and 有以為 turn on 以 with its object dropped, 'that on account of which' — nothing it acts for, or something it acts for; the whole ladder is graded by how much purpose has crept into the acting. Fourth, 其 four times over at the end with nothing to refer back to — 處其厚, 'dwell in its thick' — where English can only say 'the thick'; this weak 其 is common and is not a slip. Fifth, and worth stopping on because it comes back constantly: in 莫之應 the object pronoun 之 stands in front of its verb. That is the rule in Classical Chinese — after a negative like 莫, 不 or 未, a pronoun object moves ahead of the verb, where a noun object would not.\n\nThe descent 道 → 德 → 仁 → 義 → 禮 is the book's sharpest stroke against the Confucian vocabulary, and its sharpness is that it does not deny those virtues but ranks them, each one the symptom of what has already been lost. Then the ritualist, having got no answer, bares his arm and drags people into place: ceremony ends in force. 華 and 實 close it, flower against fruit — the showy thing that comes out early against the solid thing that comes late — and 去彼取此, 'put that away, take this', is the formula that also closes chapters 12 and 72.\n\nTextual note: the Mawangdui manuscripts do not have the 下德為之而有以為 clause at all, which has led some readers to think it a later addition made to fill out the pattern; on that reading the ladder runs straight from 上德 to 上仁.",
